{{#is_alert}}No metrics have arrived from switch-core for 15 minutes. Either the server is down, it can't reach the collector, or reporting was turned off.

**Check:** whether the pods are running and serving. If they are, the collector or the telemetry relay is the problem.

Every other alert goes quiet at the same time, so this one explains their silence.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: switch-core has stopped reporting.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}switch-core can't reach its database. Users can't do anything that reads or writes data.

**Check:** the database instance's status, and the pods' readiness.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the database is unreachable.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Requests timed out waiting for a database connection. Users see slow or failed actions.

**Check:** pool in use on the Signal board; a long query or a transaction left open usually holds the pool.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the database is refusing connections.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}On pod {{pod_name.name}}, every reporting interval for the last 10 minutes peaked with the whole database pool in use, so it keeps running into overflow. Requests slow down next, then start timing out.

**Check:** what holds connections: a slow query, a transaction left open, or a burst of work. Raise the pool size only once you know.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the database pool keeps hitting its limit.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The listener that delivers room messages is down. Messages are saved but don't reach anyone until it comes back.

**Check:** the pods' logs for the message listener; a restart brings it back while you look for why.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: room messages are not being delivered.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}More than 5 deliveries failed in 10 minutes. Some people or agents are missing messages.

**Check:** the delivery errors in the logs; one bad room can't stop the others, so look for which rooms.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are failing to deliver.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}More than 5 messages failed to save in 10 minutes. They are lost: the sender sees nothing, the room stays quiet.

**Check:** database health first, then the send errors in the logs; `kind` there says which sends failed.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are failing to send.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}At least one configured messaging app bridge (Slack, Discord, Teams...) has stopped. People on that platform are cut off from their rooms; the rest of Switch works.

**Check:** switch.bridges.running by platform on the Signal board, and "Bridge ... crashed" in the logs.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: a messaging app bridge is down.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The {{platform.name}} bridge "{{bridge_name.name}}" has crashed. People in that workspace are cut off from their rooms; the rest of Switch works.

**Check:** "Bridge ... crashed" in the logs for that bridge, then restart it or, if nobody uses it any more, deactivate it.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the {{platform.name}} bridge "{{bridge_name.name}}" runs again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}At least 10 requests failed with a server error in 10 minutes. Users see errors in the Console, Gateway or agents.

**Check:** server errors by route on the core service health dashboard, then that route's logs.

A count, not a percentage: at pilot's traffic one failure among a few requests would read as a high error rate.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: server errors are elevated.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The slowest 5% of requests took over 2 seconds, for 10 minutes. Everything feels sluggish.

**Check:** the database pool and event-loop lag on the Signal board; slowness usually starts there.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the API is slow.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The slowest 5% of messages took over 10 seconds to reach their recipients, for 15 minutes. Conversations stall.

**Check:** event-loop lag and the database pool on the Signal board.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are arriving late.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A switch-core process has stalled for over a second at a time, for 10 minutes. Every room and bridge on that pod waits while it does.

**Check:** CPU on the pods and the slowest database queries; one blocking call stalls everything.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the server is stalling.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent connections fell by more than half in 10 minutes. Agents that lost theirs stop answering in their rooms.

**Check:** pod restarts and agent connection logs; a pod loss makes its agents reconnect elsewhere.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: agent connections are dropping en masse.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_no_data}}The 09:00 report of switch-core hasn't posted to this channel for over 25 hours, so a quiet morning here doesn't mean all is well.

**Check:** the switch-daily-report CronJob's last job in switch-staging (its log says why), and that its Parameter Store settings under /switch/staging/daily-report/ are in place.{{/is_no_data}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the morning report posted again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}More than 20% of auto-route decisions in the last 15 minutes got no usable answer, counted against at least 10 decisions so that one or two failures in a quiet room don't fire. People's untagged messages reach nobody, or the triage agent, and rooms get an "auto-route is unavailable" notice.

**Check:** decision failures by reason and backend errors by kind. `timeout` or `unreachable` on the server default points at the decision service; on `endpoint`, at one workspace's own endpoint. The decision record logged for each message names the room.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: decisions are failing.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The server's default decision service has failed its health check for 5 minutes. Auto-route can't pick an agent in workspaces that use the server default, so their untagged messages reach nobody, or the triage agent. switch-core itself stays in service: this check never takes a pod out.

**Check:** the decision service's pod, not switch-core's: whether it runs, restarts or is ready, its logs, and the model server behind it.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the default decision service is unhealthy.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For 5 minutes the oldest message waiting for an auto-route decision has been over a minute old. People are waiting for an answer to start, and a message still waiting at the pending limit (two minutes by default) is dropped as expired.

**Check:** decision duration and failures: a slow or timing-out backend backs the queue up. If decisions look healthy, the decision sweep in switch-core's logs.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: undecided messages are backing up.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}At least one untagged message waited past the pending limit (two minutes by default) without a decision and was dropped. Each one is a question nobody answered.

**Check:** whether "undecided messages are backing up" or "the default decision service is unhealthy" fired too. If neither did, a switch-core restart with messages waiting is the usual cause. The decision record logged for each expired message names the room.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: decisions are expiring.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_warning}}The slowest 5% of auto-route decisions took over 1.5 seconds, for 30 minutes. People wait longer for an answer to start; nothing is lost. A warning only: decisions have a target of under a second, so this tells you without paging.

**Check:** decision duration beside model time, by backend. If model time is slow too, the model or its host is; if only duration is, the network or the backend's queue.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_warning}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: decisions are slow.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A request to switch-core raised an error nothing handled. Whoever sent it (Console, an agent, a bridge) got a 500 and whatever they were doing did not happen.

**Check:** the log sample below has the stack trace; Error Tracking groups repeats of the same one. A burst right after a deploy points at the release.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: requests are failing with unhandled errors.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A switch-core process stopped renewing its lease without shutting down (a pod shutting down in a deploy does not fire this), so the agents connected through it no longer count as connected. Their people see them go offline until they reconnect to another pod.

**Check:** whether a pod restarted or was killed (OOM, node drain) at that time, and the database pool on the Saturation panels: a process that cannot reach the database cannot renew.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: a switch-core pod lost its hold on its agents.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}switch-core kept failing to post messages to Slack, Discord or another messaging app for 10 minutes. People there do not see what agents and others wrote in Switch.

**Check:** the log names the bridge; then its platform's status page, and whether the bridge's token was revoked. One message retrying every 5 s for under a minute stays below this limit.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are not reaching a messaging app.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}switch-core logged more than 25 errors in 15 minutes, far above its usual handful. Something that is not covered by the other alerts is failing.

**Check:** the pattern list on the signal board's "Digging into error logs" section shows which message it is.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: switch-core is logging a burst of errors.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent controllers opened more than 900 connections in 30 minutes: the reconnect storm getting worse. Pilot runs at about 570 day to day because of the reconnect loop we know of; lower this to 450 once that is fixed. While it lasts, Console can show agents as Connecting and messages to them wait for the next connection.

**Check:** `event` and `reason` on `switch.controllers.connections` say how connections open and end (taken over by another pod, heartbeat lapsed); the controller log on an affected machine says what it saw. A rollout reconnects every controller once, which stays well below this limit.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: agent controllers keep reconnecting.{{/is_recovery}}

